  "source_evidence": [
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 27, printed page 5, Section 1.2.1, opening paragraph and equation (1.11)",
      "anchor": "“the hyperbolic equation together with special initial data”; “piecewise constant with a single jump discontinuity”",
      "observation": "The selected passage defines a Riemann problem by combining a hyperbolic equation with two constant initial states separated at a single spatial point, chosen as x = 0. Equation (1.11) specifies q(x,0) = q_l for x < 0 and q(x,0) = q_r for x > 0."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 23, printed page 1, paragraph introducing equation (1.1) and paragraph immediately following it",
      "anchor": "“one space dimension”; “q : R × R → R^m”; “A is a constant m × m real matrix”",
      "observation": "The introductory setting uses time-dependent unknowns with spatial coordinate x and time coordinate t. The constant-coefficient linear system is q_t(x,t) + A q_x(x,t) = 0, with an m-component real state."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 25, printed page 3, opening paragraph",
      "anchor": "“hyperbolic if the matrix A has real eigenvalues and a corresponding set of m linearly independent eigenvectors”",
      "observation": "For the constant-coefficient linear system (1.1), hyperbolicity requires real eigenvalues and a full set of linearly independent eigenvectors. The eigenvalues describe wave propagation speeds."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 25, printed page 3, Section 1.1, equations (1.8) and (1.9), continuing onto raw PDF page 26, printed page 4",
      "anchor": "“q_t(x,t) + f(q(x,t))_x = 0”; “flux Jacobian matrix f′(q)”; “nonlinear conservation laws”",
      "observation": "The chapter also considers conservation laws with flux f and a quasilinear form involving f′(q). It relates hyperbolicity to the previously stated matrix conditions and includes nonlinear fluxes, so the later definition of a Riemann problem is not restricted to constant-coefficient linear systems."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 26, printed page 4, Section 1.1.2, continuing onto raw PDF page 27, printed page 5",
      "anchor": "“provided that q and f(q) are sufficiently smooth”; “At a discontinuity in q, the partial differential equation (1.8) does not hold in the classical sense”",
      "observation": "The surrounding discussion distinguishes classical differential equations in smooth regions from the integral conservation law, which remains fundamental for discontinuous solutions."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 27, printed page 5, Section 1.2.1, paragraph following equation (1.11)",
      "anchor": "“q_l = Q_{i−1} and q_r = Q_i”; “typically a similarity solution, a function of x/t alone”",
      "observation": "The source describes using neighboring cell averages as the two initial states to obtain numerical-flux information. It also describes typical Riemann solutions as depending on x/t and consisting of finitely many waves propagating away from the origin with constant wave speeds."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 27, printed page 5, final sentence, continuing onto raw PDF page 28, printed page 6, first two paragraphs",
      "anchor": "“For linear hyperbolic systems”; “This simple structure also holds for nonlinear systems of equations”; “approximate Riemann solvers”",
      "observation": "The discussion treats both linear and nonlinear Riemann problems. It points to Chapter 3 for linear solutions, Chapters 11 and 13 for nonlinear theory, and Section 15.3 for approximate solvers; these are subsequent developments rather than premises of equation (1.11)."
    }
  ],
