  "contract_plain_english": "A Riemann problem is a hyperbolic equation together with initial data consisting of two constant states separated at a single spatial point. With that point chosen as zero, q(x,0) equals q_l for every x < 0 and q_r for every x > 0; the displayed formula does not specify q(0,0) [printed p. 5, Section 1.2.1 and equation (1.11)]. The setting includes scalar and vector-valued hyperbolic problems, and the surrounding discussion includes both linear and nonlinear systems [printed p. 1, text following equation (1.1); printed pp. 5–6, Section 1.2.1]. The phrase “single jump discontinuity” suggests unequal states, but the formula contains no explicit inequality between them [printed p. 5, opening paragraph and equation (1.11)]. This is a definition of the problem and its initial data; the subsequent qualified description of typical similarity solutions is not an unconditional solution theorem [printed p. 5, paragraph following equation (1.11)]."
